  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The software tool \"wsgriggs2/rt_fUS_BMI: v0.1.0\" is a pioneering release in the realm of real-time functional ultrasound brain-machine interfaces (fUS-BMI). With 66 downloads and 351 views, the tool marks an important step for researchers interested in decoding brain activity. Currently, it has seen proven reuse in at least one known biomedical publication, highlighting its immediate relevance to ongoing academic work.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Released in 2023 by Whitney Griggs, the software provides a stable version of real-time fUS-BMI code, aimed at enhancing research methodologies in both neuroscience and engineering. The code is openly accessible under a green open access model, fostering collaboration and innovation within the scientific community. Its deployment in research settings emphasizes its utility in decoding methods and algorithm development.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Despite the early stages of this software's adoption\u2014reflected in its current citation count of zero\u2014its download metrics suggest growing interest among scholars. The tool's educational adoption is notable, as it has already been cataloged in WorldCat, indicating it is available to a broader academic audience. This positions it potentially for educational use in relevant courses or workshops.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Furthermore, the dissemination of this tool through the Zenodo platform ensures that it is part of a broader repository that aligns with diverse subjects ranging from engineering sciences to life sciences. As it continues to gain traction, further mentions in the literature could significantly enhance its profile and establish it as a key resource in the field of brain-computer interfaces.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The existing connections to related software and articles underscore the need for continued development and exploration of this tool. As researchers increasingly look to integrate sophisticated fUS-BMI technologies in experimental designs, the adaptability and reliability of this software release may become central to advancing both theoretical and practical applications in neuroscience.</p>",
